{
  "A": "Research a serial radix replacement for bucket_sort: iterative MSD sorting of indices using unsigned-byte histograms, exact prefix-sum ranges, and one reusable scratch array. Treat NUL as a terminal bucket so shorter strings and common prefixes retain strcmp ordering without reading uninitialized padding. Skip redistribution across shared prefixes; use insertion sort only for measured small-range cutoffs. Preserve original token bytes and multiplicity. Do not assume decimal-only keys, equal token lengths, balanced buckets, or a particular input size. Deliver a complete correctness-checked program with end-to-end measurements.",
  "B": "Research an independent bounded-complexity comparison sorter: bottom-up mergesort of indices with strcmp-compatible comparisons and reusable scratch storage. Explore up to four balanced runs partitioned by record count, followed by merges; avoid assigning work solely by first-character bucket because skew can defeat load balancing. Compare serial execution with two and four workers only if threading is supported by the unchanged build; otherwise retain the serial alternative. Use size-dependent overhead cutoffs rather than test-specific dispatch. Preserve complete tokens and handle duplicates without losing records.",
  "C": "Research streaming I/O improvements in load and save: replace per-token fscanf with buffered fread parsing and per-record printf with chunked fwrite output. Correctly handle whitespace, buffer-boundary crossings, header values, and the final token at EOF. Retain token bytes, lengths, leading zeros, and one output newline per token; never convert keys to numeric values. Use contiguous storage and overflow-safe allocation/index arithmetic without assuming seekable stdin or loading the entire input file at once. Keep interfaces compatible with A/B. If retaining the baseline sorter for standalone evaluation, replace its unsafe bucket placement with counted prefix-sum ranges. Measure whole-program performance.",
  "G": "Integrate programs actually produced by A/B/C. Treat A and B as competing sorting backends; first evaluate their standalone results, then promising combinations with C's buffered I/O. Check storage lifetime, string termination, index widths, scratch ownership, and output formatting at integration boundaries. Re-run the unchanged correctness and timing evaluation for every integrated artifact within the existing budget. Select the fastest fully correct measured program, retaining standalone winners when integration regresses; never infer additive speedups. Do not alter compiler, target, tests, oracle, permissions, or budgets.",
  "analysis": "Only Problem D and src/bucketsort.c are relevant. The baseline passes four reported cases and averages 0.414560592 seconds across three close timings; there is no phase profile or workload-size evidence. Its insertion sorts have quadratic bucket cost, while fscanf and printf add per-record overhead. These are hypotheses to measure, not established bottleneck shares. The bucket layout assumes balanced occupancy and can overlap or leave holes; replacements must allocate ranges from actual counts or eliminate that layout. Preserve the source's strcmp-compatible lexicographic ordering and original token text, not an assumed numeric ordering. The target is four-core ARM64 without GPUs, favoring portable radix/comparison algorithms, supported CPU parallelism, and buffered I/O. All approaches must remain correct for every valid input without specializing to benchmark contents."
}